datalad need to keep track of things elsewhere and I risk to mess thigns up with git commit?","files":null},{"type":6,"author":{"id":"a29d5859530e551c9c63051e6b73ecbf9e195803"},"timestamp":1576089999,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDE1OlVzZXJDb250ZW50RWRpdDMyMzQwNjcyNQ=="},"target":"3c0ceb2024d331f46e4830d56976f0976e1d58a73ea8f77cd174200ca45d9572","message":"Can I use `git commit` to proceed for now or does datalad need to keep track of things elsewhere and I risk to mess things up with git commit?","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"607ac2a9afabc0e44debe80e34283e413e009770"},"timestamp":1576090044,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDU2NDY4MDEwNg==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/3919#issuecomment-564680106"},"message":"Yes, it's safe to use `git commit`.","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"607ac2a9afabc0e44debe80e34283e413e009770"},"timestamp":1576170077,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDU2NDcxMDc5MA==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/3919#issuecomment-564710790"},"message":"\u003e Anything else I can check?\n\nI suspect the most effective way to troubleshoot this would be to step through the execution of `datalad.support.gitrepo.GitRepo._diffstatus` and look at how it handles that subdataset path.  (It is essentially deciding the state based on looking at the output of `ls-files --stage -d -m [...]`,  `ls-tree -lr [..] HEAD`, and `ls-tree -m [...]`.)  Perhaps the issue is elsewhere, but that's where I'd look first.  But since only you have a repo that triggers this (though perhaps not any longer if you've call `git commit` directly :/), that's a good amount of work on your end.\n\nIt might be helpful to look at verbose debug output (e.g., `export DATALAD_LOG_LEVEL=9`), but that's going to be a flood of information, and I don't think I personally would have much luck extracting the important details from it.","files":null},{"type":6,"author":{"id":"607ac2a9afabc0e44debe80e34283e413e009770"},"timestamp":1576170077,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDE1OlVzZXJDb250ZW50RWRpdDMyNDAxODgxNQ=="},"target":"5c62fe4e1167e8eea48fadb1de90cb4352d175646bdc541e5f6b8475ef5f4a6b","message":"\u003e Anything else I can check?\n\nI suspect the most effective way to troubleshoot this would be to step through the execution of `datalad.support.gitrepo.GitRepo._diffstatus` and look at how it handles that subdataset path.  (It is essentially deciding the state based on looking at the output of `ls-files --stage -d -m [...]`,  `ls-tree -lr [..] HEAD`, and `ls-files -m [...]`.)  Perhaps the issue is elsewhere, but that's where I'd look first.  But since only you have a repo that triggers this (though perhaps not any longer if you've call `git commit` directly :/), that's a good amount of work on your end.\n\nIt might be helpful to look at verbose debug output (e.g., `export DATALAD_LOG_LEVEL=9`), but that's going to be a flood of information, and I don't think I personally would have much luck extracting the important details from it.","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"a29d5859530e551c9c63051e6b73ecbf9e195803"},"timestamp":1576175433,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDU2NTEyODA2NQ==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/3920#issuecomment-565128065"},"message":"@kyleam  and others. I think what might have happened is this:\n\nBefore the problem emerged, I tried to delete git history because I thought the .git folder was keeping 9Gb of long history for nothing. I followed the solution found here:\nhttps://stackoverflow.com/questions/13716658/how-to-delete-all-commit-history-in-github\n```\ngit checkout --orphan latest_branch\ngit add -A\ngit commit -am \"commit message\"\ngit branch -D master\ngit branch -m master\n```\n\nThis might have caused some sort of undetached HEAD state, which now I still see when running `datalad update --merge` on a sibling.\n```\n[INFO   ] Submodule HEAD got detached. Resetting branch master to point to c63c290a. Original location was f1431e2b\n```\n\nI will check online if there is a solution, but before doing that I wanted to ask if you know of a solution to resolve all the potential head deatched states in nested submodules easily.","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"607ac2a9afabc0e44debe80e34283e413e009770"},"timestamp":1576187208,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDU2NTIwMTUxNQ==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/3920#issuecomment-565201515"},"message":"\u003e I think what might have happened is this:\n\nFor clarity: I believe you're referring to is gh-3919, which is about `git status` reporting a deleted submodule while `datalad status` does not.  The misbehavior reported in this issue, which was spawned by that one, is that `datalad remove` does not commit the removal of a subdataset, which AFAICT is a consistent bug that's present on both the 0.11.x maintenance branch and the master branch.\n\n\u003e Before the problem emerged, I tried to delete git history because I thought the .git folder was keeping 9Gb of long history for nothing. [...]\n\nThanks for providing more details.  I'd very much like to figure out what was going on in gh-3919.\n\nHmm, at first glance, I don't see anything in the procedure above that seems like it'd make `git status` and `datalad status` disagree.  Quickly testing I was unable to trigger the issue from gh-3919 using the setup.\n\n\u003e ```\n\u003e [INFO   ] Submodule HEAD got detached. Resetting branch master to point to c63c290a. Original location was f1431e2b\n\u003e ```\n\nThis message typically isn't a cause for alarm.  When installing a subdataset, DataLad clones it down and then runs `git submodule update ...` to put the subdatset on the same commit that is registered in the superproject.  This often leads to a detached HEAD.  If DataLad notices that the detached HEAD is in the history of the branch it was on when it initially cloned the repo, it guesses that that's the desired branch and moves that branch to the registered commit.","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"a29d5859530e551c9c63051e6b73ecbf9e195803"},"timestamp":1576188441,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDU2NTIwNDYyNQ==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/3920#issuecomment-565204625"},"message":"Thank you @kyleam . I just found a similar conclusion as yours online (post [here](https://stackoverflow.com/questions/18770545/why-is-my-git-submodule-head-detached-from-master/55570998)). This is good, we are now starting to implement the collaborative plan where each will have their own siblings and we will merge and update each other's siblings. Hopefully this will go smoothly.","files":null},{"type":6,"author":{"id":"a29d5859530e551c9c63051e6b73ecbf9e195803"},"timestamp":1576188441,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDE1OlVzZXJDb250ZW50RWRpdDMyNDE3NDc4Mg=="},"target":"bfd93c2a29508d6db989529a63d77ebc04c5e29b8adadeb40188bbd52921380c","message":"Thank you @kyleam . I just found a similar conclusion as yours online (post [here](https://stackoverflow.com/questions/18770545/why-is-my-git-submodule-head-detached-from-master/55570998)). This is good, we are now starting to implement the collaborative plan where each will have their own copy of dataset and we will merge and update each other's siblings. Hopefully this will go smoothly.","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"607ac2a9afabc0e44debe80e34283e413e009770"},"timestamp":1576188647,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDU2NTIwOTc4OQ==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/3920#issuecomment-565209789"},"message":"I said\n\u003e `remove` does not commit the removal of a subdataset on either master or 0.11.x. That is inconsistent with how `remove` behaves with non-dataset items (unless `--nosave` is given).\n\nOy, I should have considered that it was by design.  If you specify the current dataset with `-d.`, it is saved (and the code suggests that's a deliberate choice).","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"607ac2a9afabc0e44debe80e34283e413e009770"},"timestamp":1576278300,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDU2NTY0Mjg4NQ==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/3920#issuecomment-565642885"},"message":"I said\n\u003e If you specify the current dataset with `-d.`, it is saved (and the code suggests that's a deliberate choice).\n\nAnd given that, I supose it's fine to close this and let inter-command consistency be handled as part of a larger push (e.g., #3905).","files":null},{"type":4,"author":{"id":"607ac2a9afabc0e44debe80e34283e413e009770"},"timestamp":1576278301,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDExOkNsb3NlZEV2ZW50Mjg4MjkwNzU3OA=="},"status":2},{"type":5,"author":{"id":"607ac2a9afabc0e44debe80e34283e413e009770"},"timestamp":1576278324,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDE0OlVubGFiZWxlZEV2ZW50Mjg4MjkwODE1NQ=="},"added":[],"removed":["severity-normal"]},{"type":5,"author":{"id":"607ac2a9afabc0e44debe80e34283e413e009770"},"timestamp":1576278324,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOkxhYmVsZWRFdmVudDI4ODI5MDgxNTc="},"added":["UX"],"removed":[]}]}